'''Upupoideæ''' (ū-pụ̄-poi′dē-ē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Upupa'' + ''-oideæ.''] A superfamily of tenuirostral picarian birds, approaching the passerines in many respects, but most nearly related to the hornbills, containing both the terrestrial and the arboricole hoopoes (not the plume-birds: see ''Epimachinæ''). The group is peculiar to the Old World, and is chiefly African. There are 2 families, ''Upupidæ'' and ''Irrisoridæ.''

'''upwafted''' (up-wȧf′ted), ''a.'' Borne up; carried aloft with a waving or undulatory motion. ''Cowper'', Iliad, viii.

'''upwall''' (up-wâl′), ''v. t.'' [ME. ''upwallen; < up'' + ''wall''<sup>1</sup>.] To wall up; inclose with a wall. ''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 17.

'''upward''' (up′wärd), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ME. *''upward'', < AS. ''upweard'', upward, upright, < ''up'', up, + ''-weard'' = E. ''-ward''. Cf. ''upward, adv.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' Directed or turned to a higher place; having an ascending direction, literally or figuratively.
{{smaller block|Thus far our fortune keeps an ''upward'' course.
{{right|''Shak.'', 3 Hen. VI., v. 3. 1.}}
About her feet were little beagles seen,
That watch’d with ''upward'' eyes the motions of their queen.
{{right|''Dryden'', Pal. and Arc., l. 1254.}}}}
'''Upward irrigation.''' {{smaller|See ''irrigation.''}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' The top; the height. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|The extremest ''upward'' of thy head.
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, v. 3. 136.}}}}

