'''upward, upwards''' (up′wärd, -wärdz), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''upward'', ''uppard'', also ''upwardes'', < AS. *''upweard'', ''upweardes'' (= D. ''opwaarts'' = MLG. ''upwart'', ''upwort'', also ''upwordes'' = G. ''aufwärts''), < ''up'', up, + ''-weard'' = E. ''-ward''. Cf. ''upward'', ''a.''] '''1.''' Toward a higher place; in an ascending course: opposed to ''downward''.
{{smaller block|<poem>This Nicholas sat ay as stille as stoon,
And ever gaped ''upward'' into the eir.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Miller’s Tale, l. 287.}}}}
{{smaller block|I felt to his knees, and they were as cold as any stone; and so ''upward'' and ''upward'', and all was as cold as any stone. {{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., ii. 3. 27.}}}}
'''2.''' Toward heaven and God.
{{smaller block|Criȝinge ''vpward'' to Crist and to his clene moder.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (A), v. 262.}}}}
{{smaller block|Whose mind should always, as the fire, aspire ''upwards'' to heavenly things.
{{right|''Sir T. More'', Life of Picus (Int. to Utopia, p. lxxvii.).}}}}
'''3.''' With respect to the higher part; in the upper parts.
{{smaller block|''Upward'' man, and downward fish. {{right|''Milton.''}}}}
'''4.''' Toward the source or origin: as, trace the stream ''upward''.
{{smaller block|And trace the muses ''upward'' to their spring.
{{right|''Pope'', tr. of Statius’s Thebaid, i.}}}}
'''5.''' More: used indefinitely.
{{smaller block|Children of th[e] age of .xii. or .xiii. yeares or ''vppewarde'' are diuided into two companyes, whereof the one breake the stones into smaule pieces, and the other cary furth that which is broken.
{{right|''R. Eden'', tr. of Diodorus Siculus (First Books on America, [ed. Arber, p. 369).}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>I am a very foolish fond old man,
Fourscore and ''upward''. ''Shak.'', Lear, iv. 7. 61.</poem>}}

